{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B058",
        "laureates": [
          "Albrecht Karle",
          "Christian Spiering",
          "Francis Halzen"
        ],
        "citation": "for the discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos and the opening of neutrino astronomy"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B051",
    "B007"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. IceCube joined a purpose-built, difficult detector to a distinct physical discovery: a high-energy extraterrestrial neutrino flux, subsequently strengthened by source associations. This is not simply an improved measurement but the opening of a durable astronomical messenger channel. The cumulative evidence is particularly important: it establishes a continuing observational programme rather than placing the case on a single striking event. Karle, Spiering, and Halzen remain a plausible compact allocation across detector realization, scientific vision, and collaboration leadership, provided that those roles withstand historical review.",
    "case_against": "The principal objection remains attribution. IceCube is a large, long-lived international collaboration, and a three-person award necessarily excludes many indispensable contributions in instrumentation, deployment, calibration, analysis, and interpretation. The supplied record does not itself establish that these three are the uniquely most defensible representatives. B051 offers markedly cleaner attribution and mature independent realization; B007 offers an equally profound new observational capability. These are serious comparative advantages, not merely procedural reservations.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson gives B007 priority because horizon-scale black-hole imaging made strong-gravity structure directly observable, while recognizing that its three-person attribution is difficult for a distributed collaboration.",
        "response": "I agree that B007 is an extraordinary and direct empirical achievement, and Danielsson is right that later horizon-scale observations matter in showing a programme beyond one image. I retain B058 because its diffuse flux and source evidence establish a wholly new messenger channel through several mutually reinforcing measurements. The attribution problem is not smaller for B007, so it cannot by itself decide between the two observational cases."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that B051 has mature, reproducible precision physics and a comparatively compact Katori-Ye attribution, with the citation limited to the magic-wavelength lattice-clock advance.",
        "response": "I find this the strongest alternative case. The proposed boundary for B051 is appropriately narrow, and repeated implementation gives it exceptional maturity. I nevertheless prefer IceCube because it couples a new measurement capability to discovery of a previously inaccessible cosmic channel; that scientific opening has grown into a durable body of evidence. The clearer attribution of B051 remains a reason to require unusually careful historical review before retaining the IceCube trio."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes that B056 opened routine, atomic-scale materials measurement through aberration-corrected electron optics, while requiring scrutiny of the line from corrector theory to practical implementation.",
        "response": "I agree with Olsson's instrument-builder's standard: a prizeworthy measurement technology should demonstrate a reliable new observational reach and its credit must follow the original invention rather than later refinement. B056 plainly merits that comparison. IceCube has the additional feature that the new instrument directly disclosed a new astrophysical population and channel, though its own collaboration-scale allocation must meet the same demanding attribution standard."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Karle, Spiering, and Halzen are the most historically defensible three-person representation of the IceCube collaboration.",
      "Whether the proposed citation can remain confined to discovery of the astrophysical neutrino flux and the opening of neutrino astronomy, without absorbing later collective advances in source identification.",
      "Whether the cleaner individual attribution of B051 should outweigh IceCube's direct discovery of a new astronomical channel."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No personal, institutional, or stated collaboration connection to the proposed IceCube laureates appears in the supplied profile. My astroparticle-physics expertise is relevant subject-matter experience, not a disclosed conflict."
  }
}
